---
import ProseLayout from '../layouts/ProseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site';
---

<ProseLayout
  title="Cookies"
  metaTitle="Cookie policy"
  description="This site sets no cookies. What it stores locally, why that is not a cookie, and what would change if advertising were introduced."
  path="/cookies"
  lastUpdated="2026-08-20"
  keywords={['cookie policy', 'cookies']}
>
  <p>
    <strong>This site sets no cookies.</strong> Not functional, not analytics, not advertising. That is
    the whole policy as things currently stand, but the rest of this page explains what the site does
    store, why that is not a cookie, and what would change if that ever did.
  </p>

  <h2>What is stored instead</h2>

  <p>
    Two preferences are kept in your browser's local storage, so the site remembers how you like it:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>theme</strong> — light or dark appearance.</li>
    <li><strong>units</strong> — metric or imperial.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Local storage differs from a cookie in the way that matters here: it is never attached to
    network requests. A cookie is sent to the server with every request, which is what makes cookies
    useful for tracking. Local storage stays on your device and is readable only by this site's own
    scripts. Neither value identifies you, and clearing your browser data removes both.
  </p>

  <p>
    Under the EU ePrivacy Directive, storage that is strictly necessary for a service the user has
    explicitly requested does not require consent. A theme preference you set by pressing the theme
    button falls squarely in that category, which is why there is no consent banner on this site.
  </p>

  <h2>Third-party cookies</h2>

  <p>
    There are none, because there are no third parties. The site makes no requests to any other
    domain: no analytics, no tag manager, no advertising network, no embedded video, no social
    widgets, and no external font service. Fonts are served from this domain precisely so that
    loading a page contacts nobody else.
  </p>

  <h2>If advertising is introduced later</h2>

  <p>
    The site is built to allow advertising to be added without a redesign, and the honest thing is
    to describe what that would mean rather than leave it unsaid.
  </p>

  <p>If ads are ever enabled:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      An advertising provider would set cookies and similar identifiers, used for ad delivery,
      frequency capping, measurement and, unless limited, personalisation.
    </li>
    <li>
      A consent mechanism would be activated before any advertising script loads. It ships with the
      site already, switched off, and it gates the scripts rather than merely recording a choice
      after the fact.
    </li>
    <li>
      For visitors in the EEA and UK, that mechanism must be a certified Consent Management Platform
      under Google's requirements before personalised ads may be served, and it must offer refusal
      as easily as acceptance.
    </li>
    <li>
      This page, the <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> and the consent mechanism would all be updated
      before anything went live, not after.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    None of that is active today. Nothing on this page describes something currently happening; it
    describes what would have to happen first.
  </p>

  <h2>Controlling storage</h2>

  <p>
    Every browser can clear local storage through its privacy or site-data settings, and most let
    you block it per site. Blocking it here costs you nothing except that the site forgets your
    theme and unit preferences between visits. The calculators work regardless.
  </p>

  <p>
    Questions: <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>.
  </p>
</ProseLayout>
